# Judged losses. WARRANT :: CONTENT — see prove-no-loss.sh --help for the
# closed set of warrants. Entries match line CONTENT as a substring and expire
# the moment the line changes, which is when they need re-judging. An entry
# matching more than one line, or shorter than 8 characters, is refused.
#
# A warrant is SINGLE-TRANSITION, unlike .skills/context-seams-ok. A seam recurs
# on every run, so its acknowledgement earns its place indefinitely. A warranted
# loss justifies one base -> now comparison: once that change is merged, every
# later run's --base sits after it and the line never appears again. So an entry
# is spent the moment the change it warrants lands, and prune it then — leaving
# it behind means every future run reports it as "matched nothing", which is the
# same signal a genuinely stale entry gives, and the file stops being readable.
#
# (The 14 entries that justified the #95 trim were removed on merge for exactly
# that reason — CR round 2, finding 9. The trim itself is in git history.)
#
# The file is per-repo while --file is per-target, so an entry for one target
# used to report "matched nothing" on a run against another. Scope it instead:
# `PATH :: WARRANT :: CONTENT` is consulted only by runs whose --file contains
# PATH, and the report says how many entries sat a run out (#139).


